Harry writes:
> Tim Writes:
> >As for disturbing patterns, in a kind of ersatz Heisenbergian sense, I
> >am more skeptical that this is a major issue. Too many competing
> >models, too much apparent noise in the system, too many heterogeneous
> >agents. 
> 
> It would be sorta fun, although probally to expensive, to try to detect
> other computer trading programs and to try to find weaknesses in them,
> and thus earn money off of their flaws. (e.g., ignore the market,
> per se, and just focus on trading "against" other computer traders.)

Indeed, such "contrarian" strategies are just as valid (are
first-class entities, we might say) as are seemingly more fundamental
analyses. In fact, it's nearly impossible to say some considerations
are more fundamental than others. Remember the Dutch tulip bulb
bubble? 

Nick Szabo and I have been kicking over using AIT to measure
information flows, to measure parsimony in theories ("systems" in
gambling/investing terms). The rough--and early--idea is that theories
which appear to be too simple to predict a complex system probably are
too simple.

Simple-minded notions that "trend lines" (a few bits of algorithmic
information content) and other simplistic systems ("candlestick
formations," "Elliot Wave," etc....the crapola reported in numerous
investment letters and on CNBC) are likely to be doomed. 

Simple laws, like Kepler's Laws, can emerge from incredibly confusing
data, but this is rare, and indicates the presence of deeply pervasive
laws of physics and whatnot. Such simple laws are unlikely to be found
in complex markets made up of heterogenous agents and many sources of
noisy or incomplete information. The algorithmic or descriptive
complexity of the system is just too high to admit a simple "short
program" that explains it (though we'll of course never be able to
prove such a program does not in fact exist).

All of this is not to say that heavy analysis of vast amounts of data
will not produce profits. In fact, the above Kolmogorov-based argument
(a glimpse, at least) makes it likely that the efficient market
hypothesis is itself a gross oversimplification. 

It would be as surprising to find that traders could not find profits
in such a market as to find that some animals cannot gain advantage
over other animals in a complex ecology!

(I think I just made an important observation, in a form I'd never
thought about before. Can the "efficient market hypothesis" be cast as
an "efficient ecology hypothesis" and then critiqued on that basis?
Food for thought.)

--Tim May

