David's method has been the only way I've made real money in the
market. I've frequently understood a company's products much better
than the average investor. I can talk to Phil Karn at Qualcomm (who I
happen to know from when we both worked at Bellcore) and he'll tell me
lots of interesting facts about CDMA technology. He'll happily tell
these same facts to the analysts, and he has, but they won't
understand the implications and I will because the average analyst
doesn't know spread spectrum from sandwich spread. (Sadly, I didn't
know that Qualcomm was public until it was nearly too late -- sigh.)

Remember, folks: for the market to be efficient, there have to be
people out there acting on the information that they have. If everyone
sat back and said "no point in investing, since the market is
efficient" the market would be grossly inefficient. The market can
never be perfectly efficient anyway. The smaller the company, the less
efficient the market and the more money can potentially be made.

