Problem and algorithm specification.


My claim is that a quantum computer can rapidly solve
many variable minimization problems, when there is a large
difference between the global minimum - the correct
solution - and local minima, where a classical computer
would take polynomial time, polynomial in the number of
variables.

Assume we have N bits.

We have a large number of constraints on the bits, of the
form "Bit3 or Bit 7 or Bit 5 is true."

We wish to find a value for the bits such that all the
constraints are satisfied.

This problem is of course NP complete.  However the
important special case of this problem is not NP complete,
but is NP (requires non polynomial time) for all known
algorithms.  (You can win the bet by finding a polynomial
time algorithm for it.)

We are mostly interested in solving this problem in the
case where there is a large difference between the true
solution, and the next best solution.

Digression:  This is irrelevant to the bet, but is the
reason why I am interested in this class of problem.

  Suppose the constraints represent facts about the world,
  and the bits represent the way the world actually is. 
  Yes, the perception problem.  The big unsolved problem of
  artificial intelligence, the classic "AI hard" problem.

  In that case we will have redundant constraints.  The
  chance that a solution will exist by chance alone is
  remote.

  There will be one value that satisfies the constraints,
  and all other values will violate a substantial number of
  constraints.

  The large difference between the true solution and a
  solution that just happens to be better than other
  solutions is what tells us that the world is real, not an
  arbitrary image forced onto a Rorschach blot, that our
  description of the world is not merely an arbitrary pattern
  capriciously selected from chaos, but a good
  description of the real world, objective and external 
  to ourselves.

End digression.  The issue we are betting on is not the
practical and philosophical significance of this class of
problem, but whether a quantum computer can efficiently
solve some classes of problem that have no polynomial time
solution by known methods.

Suppose we have a set of constraints such that a histogram
of the number of solutions that violate n constraints would
look something like this.

                                              **
                                            ******
        ^                                 **********
        |                                ************
                                        ***************
number of                              *****************
solutions                            *********************
that                              ***************************
violate         *             ***********************************
that many                                   80
constraints     0       
                number of constraints violated ->


Existing numerical methods will merely find *a* solution
that is in the left hand edge of the normal distribution.
To find the true solution, the solution that lies outside
the normal distribution, they need to search a significant
fraction of *all* the possible values, which of course
requires non polynomial time.

My claim is:

For a set of constraints, such that best "almost correct"
solutions violate a large number of constraints, a quantum
computer algorithm, which I will now specify, can find the
correct solution in polynomial time, whereas no known
classical algorithm can solve it in polynomial time.

Quantum computer algorithm:

Let each bit be represented by a two state degree of
freedom.

Let Ti be an operator whose eigenvalues are 1 and 0, with
the eigenvector whose eigenvalue is one corresponding to
Bit i being true, and the eigenvector whose eigenvalue is
zero corresponding to Bit i being false.

Let Ni be the negation for Bit i.  This operator maps true
to false and false to true.  Ni is the "not" operator for
Bit i.

NTN = 1-T, thus NT + TN = N

The eigenvalues of N are +1 and -1

N |true> =  |false>
N |false> = |true>


The eigenvectors of N are sqrt(1/2) ( |true> + |false> )
                          sqrt(1/2) ( |true> - |false> )

Let C be the constraint operator.  The eigenvalues of C
are the number of constraints violated, and the
eigenvectors of C are solutions that violate that many
constraints.   The eigenvectors of C have the usual
classical equivalents.  The eigenvectors of N do not.


For example if we had four constraints:
 Bit0  | Bit 1  | Bit 4  is true
 Bit1  | !Bit 2 | !Bit 4 is true
 !Bit1 | !Bit 2 | Bit 3  is true
 !Bit0 | Bit 2  | !Bit 3 is true

Then 

C =   (1-T0)*(1-T1)*(1-T4)
    + (1-T1)*  T2  *  T4
    +   T1  *  T2  *(1-T3)
    +   T0  *(1-T2)*  T3

(Typically we will have 56, 84, or 120 constraints.

The histogram above is also a histogram of the degeneracy
of the eigenvalues of C, degeneracy plotted against the
eigenvalue.

The quantum computer is prepared with each bit in 
the +1 eigenstate of N.  This of course has no classical
equivalent.

Assume we have m bits.

The Hamiltonian of the quantum computer will be:

H = a*C*cnorm(t/b) -N0 -N1 -N2 .... -Nm

Where cnorm is the cumulative normal distribution, and t
is the simulated time.

cnorm(t) = integral -infinity to t of exp(-x*x/2)

Thus the Hamiltonian goes smoothly from something that has
no classical equivalent, to a Hamiltonian that behaves in
an essentially classical manner.

Initially cnorm is very small, exponentially small, and
the initial state is very close to the ground state of
the Hamiltonian.

I predict that when cnorm is large, the system will still
be in the ground state of the Hamiltonian, but of course
the ground state now corresponds to the classical solution
of the constraint problem.

I also predict that the time required for a transition that
does not bounce the system out of its ground state will
only grow polynomially fast - that "b" is a polynomial
function of m, the number of bits.  I further predict that
b will be a low order polynomial function of m, so that
the difference between b(m) and 2^m is dramatically
obvious in the small number of bits that are accessible to
numerical experiment.

Of course I can only test this numerically for quite small
numbers of bits, because the time required to simulate such
a quantum system goes up exponentially with the number of
bits.  But I predict that the time that the simulated
system requires will go up quite slowly, making the
difference obvious when we enlarge the system by one or
two bits.  (Since polynomial time algorithms usually go as
2^n or n!)

The time required by my simulating system (the PC) will
grow as m*2^m, but the time for the system simulated will
grow slowly.  I would guess that b is proportional to m, or
proportional to m * log(m), but the bet is merely that it
is polynomial, and grows at a rate dramatically slower than
2^m or m!, which is the rate at which non polynomial
algorithms normally grow, for the relatively small number
of bits for which the algorithm can be tested numerically.

