I have never understood the reasoning behind this claim.  The market
cannot obtain and reflect new information unless someone trades on
the basis of that information.  And that someone, trading using
exclusive information, ought to have a comparative advantage over
uninformed traders -- their expectations of future value will be
different and more accurate, and so therefore will their estimations
of present market value.  The markets in options weren't efficient
before Black-Scholes, were they?

I think of it as analogous to an arbitrage operation.  If nobody
runs them, asynchrony can develop.  If somebody does, they can make
money.  No economist would claim that it is impossible to profit
from currency arbitrage, even though the currency exchange rates
tend (are driven by arbitrage!) to be neutrally interconvertible.

