Ah, I remember that book.  A number of interesting people have
attacked roulette, though blackjack would seem an easier target.
Shannon had a roulette wheel in his basement, and developed a
strategy based on the fact that a very slight non-levelness produces
a dramatic effect on the wheel's results.  Dunno if he ever used
it.  And Walford made some money at roulette before getting into
life extension.

Shannon's observation, by the way, was quite clever.  The potential
energy of a ball entering the cups (is that what they're called?) on
a tilted roulette wheel is sinusoidal, one cycle around the wheel.
The ball starts with plenty of potential energy and loses it quite
slowly.  On a flat wheel, it can land anywhere.  But on a tilted
wheel, much of the circumference is `shadowed' by the bumps in P.E.:

ball ...
        ```___..           ___
         /~   ~\ ```...  /~   ~\   
\       /       \      `*  <- ball lands
 \.___./         \.___./

	       \_______/
		   | the `shadowed' area

So then you need to track the spin of the wheel (which is quite
steady over the course of one trial) to know what numbers will
be unshadowed when the ball lands.  And house rules need to permit
you to place bets while the wheel is in motion, of course...

Real-world roulette wheels complicate things considerably, but I
guess you still have an edge.  I don't remember whether the
Eudaemonic Pie people used this observation, though they probably
did.

