Date: Sat, 30 Oct 93 14:30:10 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Beating the Stock Market?

The approach of betting against the other guy's model (or anticipating
it) assumes we know what the other guy's model is.  But most models
are kept secret.  Furthermore, those models may themselves
be contrarian -- and we run into the halting problem.  Nevertheless,
there may be dominant fads, or consistent psychological biases in
some kinds of markets.  For example, at least as of the last decade 
people who bet on long odds horses lost substantially more than those 
who bet on the favorite.  (Both lost, of course, because the house takes 
c. 17%).  The fact that the house can take so much, and that gambling
is a $40+ billion industry worldwide, even more dramatically demonstrates
such biases (which may be irrational or reflect convex utility functions, 
take your pick).  Many traders (Paul Tudor Jones, George Soros, etc.)
claim to have found & profited from such biases.  Soros & Druckenmiller
make money anticipating central banks, which like bulls in the
china shop heave large volumes with little sophistication.

Most of the fixed income synthetic assets market is based on 
(a) differences in utility functions between various market players, and 
(b) Hayek's "time and place" considerations -- some people need money 
today, others not until next year, some need money in Europe, others 
in the U.S., etc.  The wide variety of synthetic assets communicate these 
needs more efficiently than the traditional narrow fixed-income
securities.

More speculatively, I've been playing with a sociobiological theory
of utility functions, which ties in individual utility functions with 
human "selfish gene"-based motivations, testing the theories against
demographic data.  (eg, what kinds of people are mostly likely to
gamble at long odds or borrow money, what securities in what kinds
of markets are most likely to contain utility function bias, etc.)

Tim May:
> The rough--and early--idea is that theories
> which appear to be too simple to predict a complex system probably are
> too simple.

Yes, but straightforward testing on sufficient data reveals their lack.
It's much harder to accept or reject systems whose 
complexity approaches that of the data being modelled.
My main take on parsimony comes from the opposite direction -- that overly
complex models are likely to merely "postdict" the random noise.  Only
patterns simpler than the data they explain reflect an ongoing
phenomenon.  Kolmogorov complexity K (algorithmic entropy Htm in 
Chaitin's terminology) provides a good measure of this probability:  
for H(residuals)+K(x)+k = H(x), the probability that the model
is just noise is 2^-k.  The higher k, the clearer the signal.
In IT terms the market can be modeleed as a noisy communications
channel which transmits motivations, utility functions, physical
reality, technological capability, social/economic conditions, etc. 
with very lossy compression.  The goal of arbitrage is to
anticipate signals being transmitted between markets, or
more broadly defined, between other kinds of data streams (econometric, 
weather, etc.) and markets.

> Can the "efficient market hypothesis" be cast as
> an "efficient ecology hypothesis" and then critiqued on that basis?

Perhaps, but most animals don't communicate resource information
to each other, they typically have to find out for themselves.
One interesting argument against the efficient market might
be the high distortion of the compression scheme.  There is much
value in lowering the distortion, decrease the compression loss.
The fixed income synthetic assets seem to provide good examples
of such innovations.

