Date: Sat, 30 Oct 93 19:11:18 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Beating the stock market

Perry, Tim May, David Friedman, et. al. have mentioned investing in 
individual stocks based on an evaluation of the business's use of
technology (typically in their products).  No doubt this information 
eventually enters the market, either early via those closest to the 
business or later by reflection of the product's success in the company's
financial statement.  As a strategy technology analysis may average
slightly better than throwing darts, if you consistently utilize 
valuable information and distinguish popular techno-fads from
real market demands.  However, there are several problems with this 
strategy:

(1) Nobody has demonstrated they can consistently beat the market 
this way. The reports are typically anecdotal and the number of 
investments statistically insignificant.  We see quite a bit of 
selective revelation:  people who succeed tend to brag about it, 
people who don't tend to keep quiet.  We see few numbers comparing
gains to the market -- the S&P 500 has more than quadrupled since 
1982, so most dart-board investments have made major gains over 
that time.   

(2) Even assuming these investors have sufficiently unique and
important information, the method typically used to take a
advantage of it, investing in individual stocks, is crude,
often needlessly exposing the investor to risk unrelated to
the claimed information.  It is often better to hedge away  most
non-info-related risk, industry- or market-wide, with index options 
or option baskets, and achieve the desired risk/reward profile via 
margin.

(3) These strategies are often subjective, insufficiently explained
and therefore non-replicable.  This also makes it difficult to
determine risk profiles, integrate the strategy into computerized
portfolio analysis, etc.

For those with good knowledge and judgement, and amenable
to high risk profiles, the best way to use technological
knowledge may be to be one of the first investors
in a startup situation, when the number of investors is 
sufficiently small and one can obtain rare, intimate knowledge 
of the technology and market possibilities.  The
opportunties to hedge that risk are correspondingly 
lessened, however.  The ideal situation might be a 
technologically-informed VC fund that could place side
wagers to hedge some of its risk, and thus can take
a higher risk profile in its investments.

