From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: Beating the stock market

Thinking of it some time ago, I settled on a couple of Fidelity
Select funds whose specialization, according to my great long-term
visions of technology development, puts them in a favorable position
in relation to others.  This approach also allows me not to follow
details in industry development (which isn't worth my time, at least
in the particular combination of spare time, investment money and
market expertise that I have).
  The funds I picked were Biotech and Developing Communications.
Since I bought them, they had really different performance;
Biotech was jumping wildly in all directions, and now is just where
I bought it at, while Developing Communications stagnated for a long
time until it recently took off.
  So my idea was: I develop the visions; the fund managers watch the
companies.  With the hope that those humans can't possibly overestimate
perspectives of my favorite fields, so the stocks there would tend to
be consistently strategically undervalued.
   Temporarily, though, the market can fall significantly; besides,
the Biotech Select fund managers are not exactly looking at the 
companies that are more likely to develop the first commercial
nanotech products; they use advice of analysts watching the current
product development, which may well mean that the goal I made the
investment into this thing for is being missed.
Though companies that are good now in a strategically promising
area maybe more likely to better capitalize on the coming breakthroughs,
even if nobody in the market realizes it at this point.

   My main problem though is where to find the money to invest...   :-(

